00:15:22,880 --> 00:15:27,620
really mean it but simon sharma says you know how do you know how are people supposed to tell

205
00:15:27,620 --> 00:15:31,700
the difference so this is the climate on the 29th of august

206
00:15:32,580 --> 00:15:39,520
the prussians reach verdun and verdun is the keystone of france's eastern defensive line if

207
00:15:39,520 --> 00:15:44,320
you get past verdun you're into the valley of the marne and once you go through that you are heading

208
00:15:44,320 --> 00:15:52,060
towards paris and verdun surrenders after three days to this impregnable fortress yeah the garrison

209
00:15:52,060 --> 00:15:56,440
commander who'd said he'd never surrender either kills himself or is killed by the people of verdun

210
00:15:56,440 --> 00:16:01,180
who basically don't fancy a siege at all we're like yeah we'll let the prussians in fine so

211
00:16:02,580 --> 00:16:06,480
you've got a big long line of soldiers and the news arrives in paris on sunday the 2nd of september

212
00:16:06,480 --> 00:16:12,020
the prussians are broken through and now you've got i mean it's an extraordinary scene you have

213
00:16:12,020 --> 00:16:17,420
kind of church bells ringing there are cannons on the riversane sounding the alert and this is

214
00:16:17,420 --> 00:16:22,360
when don tong gives his chilean address exactly don tong gives his address there are posters

215
00:16:22,360 --> 00:16:30,320
going up across the city to arms to arms the enemy is at the gates now that afternoon we can be pretty

216
00:16:30,320 --> 00:16:32,560
sure that something else happened but we can't see them so the union is looking for an aid or something

217
00:16:32,560 --> 00:16:36,660
Can't be exactly certain what, because the documents were later destroyed.

218
00:16:36,740 --> 00:16:39,820
They were destroyed in the events of 1870-71.

219
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:40,820
It's ironic, isn't it?

220
00:16:40,920 --> 00:16:42,900
Another Prussian day trip to Paris.

221
00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:48,020
So what seems to have happened is that some of the sections, these are the kind of neighborhood

222
00:16:48,020 --> 00:16:53,140
councils in Paris, discussed how we eliminate these people in the prisons.

223
00:16:53,400 --> 00:16:57,680
And there are a lot of people saying it's an unpleasant necessity, but basically somebody

224
00:16:57,680 --> 00:16:58,480
has to do it.

225
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:02,920
Now, that's not to say the sections ordered it, but the tone has kind of been set.

226
00:17:03,100 --> 00:17:07,080
At about this point, the chief prison inspector comes to Danton in the Hôtel de Ville in

227
00:17:07,080 --> 00:17:11,200
City Hall, and he says, I'm genuinely worried about the safety of the people in the prisons.

228
00:17:11,380 --> 00:17:14,840
And Danton says, je me fous bien des prisonniers.

229
00:17:15,420 --> 00:17:18,780
I don't give a damn about the prisoners, like basically let them fend for themselves.

230
00:17:19,080 --> 00:17:22,500
But to be fair to him, I mean, one of the reasons for that is that he can't spare people

231
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:23,300
from the barricades.

232
00:17:23,460 --> 00:17:25,020
There's only a finite number of people.

233
00:17:25,060 --> 00:17:28,380
And obviously the main threat from his perspective is...

234
00:17:28,480 --> 00:17:29,740
The approach of the Prussians.

235
00:17:29,940 --> 00:17:31,320
Tom, I'm so surprised at this.

236
00:17:31,420 --> 00:17:34,120
I thought you were going to be on the other side of this equation, but it's very clear

237
00:17:34,120 --> 00:17:40,420
to me now, from the way you've conducted yourself in this whole episode, that, you know, you're

238
00:17:40,420 --> 00:17:45,360
going to take a very Dominic Sandbrook, princes in the tower line on this issue, are you?

239
00:17:45,580 --> 00:17:51,860
Well, no, but I do think that there are reasons why someone like Danton, people in charge

240
00:17:51,860 --> 00:17:57,940
of the revolution, might be unconcerned with prisoners and the security of the prisoners.

241
00:17:57,940 --> 00:18:00,480
That relates to the overall situation in Paris.

242
00:18:00,660 --> 00:18:01,300
Yeah, fair enough.

243
00:18:01,420 --> 00:18:05,700
And also don't forget, everybody thinks the prisoners are total villains and traitors and

244
00:18:05,700 --> 00:18:06,500
all of that stuff.

245
00:18:06,640 --> 00:18:07,140
Of course.

246
00:18:07,240 --> 00:18:11,100
But I think that saying, you know, we can't bother with them, it's justified by military

247
00:18:11,560 --> 00:18:11,920
exigencies.

248
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:12,380
Fair enough.

249
00:18:12,620 --> 00:18:14,000
Listeners can make up their own minds, can't they?

250
00:18:14,060 --> 00:18:15,440
I have to say Theo agrees with you.

251
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:16,220
He's written in the chat.

252
00:18:16,340 --> 00:18:16,960
Well said, Tom.

253
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:18,700
So that's nice.

254
00:18:18,860 --> 00:18:19,980
So let's get into the story.

255
00:18:20,340 --> 00:18:24,640
Sometime about 2.30 that afternoon, the 2nd of September, the news has come in from Verdun.

256
00:18:24,760 --> 00:18:27,920
There was a group of prisoners being escorted through the city from the city of Verdun.

257
00:18:27,920 --> 00:18:29,880
The Palais de Justice to the Abbey prison.

258
00:18:30,360 --> 00:18:33,820
And they are royal officials, their courtiers, and their Catholic priests.

259
00:18:34,220 --> 00:18:37,660
The streets are obviously packed with people because of the war panic.

260
00:18:37,820 --> 00:18:41,420
And a lot of people shout abuse at them as they pass in these kind of carriages.

261
00:18:42,100 --> 00:18:46,700
As they get towards the Abbey prison, which is in Saint-Germain-des-Prés, it's a former

262
00:18:46,700 --> 00:18:49,760
abbey, hence the name, a group of people stop them.

263
00:18:49,820 --> 00:18:53,500
These people are National Guardsmen, sans-culottes, a mixture of characters.

264
00:18:53,820 --> 00:18:57,900
They stop the carriages, they drag the prisoners out, and they drag them into the nearby prison

265
00:18:57,920 --> 00:18:59,840
section headquarters, which is a convent.

266
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:03,620
And there's a great crowd of people there, and the people are shouting, these are enemies

267
00:19:03,620 --> 00:19:04,240
of the people.

268
00:19:04,520 --> 00:19:05,920
You know, why take them to the prison?

269
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:07,140
Why not just get rid of them now?

270
00:19:07,940 --> 00:19:12,420
Two or three of those prisoners try to break out, try to get away, or fight for their lives

271
00:19:12,420 --> 00:19:12,780
or whatever.

272
00:19:13,260 --> 00:19:17,280
There's a scuffle, they're beaten up, and they are hacked to death with knives, and

273
00:19:17,280 --> 00:19:18,480
their bodies are left in the courtyard.

274
00:19:18,820 --> 00:19:23,340
The rest of the prisoners are just, you know, 20 people or so, are just standing there absolutely

275
00:19:23,780 --> 00:19:25,880
traumatized in shock, wondering what's going to happen.

276
00:19:26,040 --> 00:19:27,660
An impromptu tribunal.

277
00:19:27,920 --> 00:19:30,360
It's set up, and it declares them guilty.

278
00:19:30,620 --> 00:19:34,900
Then one by one, in a very methodical way, they're led down the steps into the garden,

279
00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:39,440
and there a group of men has assembled with knives, axes, hatchets.

280
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:43,000
There's a guy who's like clearly a carpenter because he's brought his saw.

281
00:19:43,300 --> 00:19:46,660
And one by one, they are hacked to death.

282
00:19:47,220 --> 00:19:50,120
It's important to say right from the beginning, this is the first incident.

283
00:19:50,360 --> 00:19:52,460
It is not a mad frenzy.

284
00:19:52,480 --> 00:19:53,960
It's not an orgy of violence.

285
00:19:54,280 --> 00:19:55,440
It is quiet.

286
00:19:56,180 --> 00:19:57,220
It's considered.

287
00:19:57,920 --> 00:19:59,220
The guys take their time.

288
00:19:59,380 --> 00:20:02,100
It takes about half an hour to kill all these men.

289
00:20:02,560 --> 00:20:03,580
And then it is done.

290
00:20:03,700 --> 00:20:08,740
And upstairs, the people who are still there, the room is absolutely full of people, and

291
00:20:08,740 --> 00:20:12,460
they are debating, now that we've killed these people, why don't we just go into the Abbe

292
00:20:12,460 --> 00:20:16,180
prison and do the same with everybody who is in there?

293
00:20:16,540 --> 00:20:20,940
But again, not necessarily to kill them, to sit in judgment on them.

294
00:20:21,080 --> 00:20:22,740
As we'll see, not everybody is killed.

295
00:20:22,800 --> 00:20:23,340
You're absolutely right.

296
00:20:24,080 --> 00:20:27,620
While that crowd is all debating and arguing about how they're going to do it.

297
00:20:27,920 --> 00:20:33,720
A separate group breaks into another prison about a mile away called the Karm, it's a former

298
00:20:33,720 --> 00:20:34,620
Karmalite convent.

299
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:38,520
So a lot of these prisons are convents and Abbes and religious houses that have been

300
00:20:38,520 --> 00:20:38,980
converted.

301
00:20:39,240 --> 00:20:41,400
In this prison, there are about 160 priests.

302
00:20:41,620 --> 00:20:44,940
This crowd, again, organizes a kind of improvised tribunal.

303
00:20:45,060 --> 00:20:47,620
They call out the prisoners' names one by one.

304
00:20:47,740 --> 00:20:49,340
They take them out into the garden.

305
00:20:49,840 --> 00:20:50,900
Some of them are shot.

306
00:20:51,240 --> 00:20:53,320
Most of them, though, are hacked to death again.

307
00:20:53,720 --> 00:20:57,280
Some of them try to climb over the walls or they even climb trees to get away.

308
00:20:57,280 --> 00:21:00,080
But they are dragged back and finished off with knives.

309
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:03,120
Again, just to say, I mean, a quarter of them are spared.

310
00:21:03,400 --> 00:21:04,020
Well, hold on.

311
00:21:04,040 --> 00:21:07,260
115 people out of 160 are killed in this.

312
00:21:07,460 --> 00:21:07,760
Absolutely.

313
00:21:07,780 --> 00:21:10,220
Because I think it's easy to think that it is a total massacre.

314
00:21:10,640 --> 00:21:12,100
Well, you wouldn't take your chances with that.

315
00:21:12,180 --> 00:21:12,640
Of course not.

316
00:21:12,720 --> 00:21:16,840
It's just really supporting what you were saying, that this isn't a kind of frothing

317
00:21:16,840 --> 00:21:19,740
at the mouth, mad mob frenzy.

318
00:21:19,860 --> 00:21:25,040
It's much more considered and therefore I think actually much more frightening than

319
00:21:25,040 --> 00:21:25,280
that.

320
00:21:25,500 --> 00:21:25,940
Agreed.

321
00:21:25,940 --> 00:21:27,820
And by the way, we both gave different figures.

322
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:30,180
That's not because one of us is right and the other is wrong.

323
00:21:30,260 --> 00:21:32,940
It's because every history book gives different figures on this.

324
00:21:33,220 --> 00:21:35,740
So yeah, there is a lot of confusion about the figures.

325
00:21:35,980 --> 00:21:40,620
Now meanwhile, the death squad has got started in the Abbe prison and also in some of the

326
00:21:40,620 --> 00:21:41,300
other prisons.

327
00:21:41,500 --> 00:21:43,480
And in all of them, it is the same kind of routine.

328
00:21:43,560 --> 00:21:45,620
I think actually death squad is better than mob.

329
00:21:46,020 --> 00:21:50,100
They're often described as mobs, but these are kind of organized teams of men.

330
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:53,180
They almost always hold this kind of tribunal.

331
00:21:53,440 --> 00:21:55,300
They bring people out into the courtyard.

332
00:21:55,940 --> 00:21:59,460
Once they've been found guilty and then they stab them or hack them to death.

333
00:21:59,580 --> 00:22:03,080
And we only know one of the names of the people who organized this.

334
00:22:03,180 --> 00:22:06,580
A guy called Stanislas Maia, who was a clerk.

335
00:22:06,760 --> 00:22:08,280
He'd been at the Bastille.

336
00:22:08,460 --> 00:22:11,480
He'd been a big figure in the women's March on Versailles.

337
00:22:11,900 --> 00:22:17,100
And he seems to have been one of those people who's thrown up by these periods of revolution

338
00:22:17,100 --> 00:22:18,140
and kind of chaos.

339
00:22:18,180 --> 00:22:19,380
So a bit of a bully.

340
00:22:19,560 --> 00:22:23,880
You could say he's the kind of person who would be a paramilitary leader and he loves

341
00:22:23,880 --> 00:22:24,240
all this.

342
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:26,300
And he's obviously, this is his, his moment.

343
00:22:26,480 --> 00:22:27,720
But it's interesting, isn't it?

344
00:22:27,720 --> 00:22:33,620
That actually so few names are known and in large part, that is because in due course

345
00:22:33,620 --> 00:22:39,960
to be a septembre as they come to be called is highly dangerous because it comes to be

346
00:22:39,960 --> 00:22:42,300
seen as a terrible blot on the reputation of the revolution.

347
00:22:42,620 --> 00:22:43,840
Not immediately though.

348
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:48,640
And I think that that reflects the fact that this is genuinely not being organized by the

349
00:22:48,640 --> 00:22:50,860
big names, by Danton or Robespierre or whatever.

350
00:22:51,020 --> 00:22:52,760
It's coming probably from the sections.

351
00:22:52,760 --> 00:22:58,240
And the people who are organizing it are not people who will go on to great things to become

352
00:22:58,240 --> 00:22:59,100
kind of famous names.

353
00:22:59,360 --> 00:23:00,500
I think you're absolutely right, Tom.

354
00:23:00,600 --> 00:23:04,960
I think the is being organized in, in so far as there is a sense of organization is at

355
00:23:04,960 --> 00:23:07,500
a very, very sort of low level.

356
00:23:07,880 --> 00:23:10,800
It's these kind of neighborhood councils, the commune.

357
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:14,880
We know that they talked about that's an instructionary council that's taking control of Paris.

358
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:19,740
We know they talked about it and they said your point, there weren't enough men to protect

359
00:23:19,740 --> 00:23:20,460
the prisons.

360
00:23:20,560 --> 00:23:22,060
We need them on the barricades.

361
00:23:22,340 --> 00:23:22,740
Yeah.

362
00:23:22,760 --> 00:23:27,080
And one of the communist committees issued a statement signed by Marat, the prisoners

363
00:23:27,080 --> 00:23:29,300
are brigands who will slaughter our children and our women.

364
00:23:29,440 --> 00:23:33,700
These acts of justice are indispensable to deter through the use of terror these legions

365
00:23:33,700 --> 00:23:34,200
of traitors.

366
00:23:34,400 --> 00:23:37,140
But it's not like Marat is being named as a guy who's leading it.

367
00:23:37,340 --> 00:23:38,360
No, no, he's not going in.

368
00:23:38,540 --> 00:23:39,380
He's inspired it.

369
00:23:39,540 --> 00:23:43,180
But I think these are basically the people who had attacked the Tuileries, right?

370
00:23:43,440 --> 00:23:44,280
Yeah, I think that's right.

371
00:23:44,600 --> 00:23:47,800
It's people from the working class areas of Paris.

372
00:23:47,960 --> 00:23:52,140
They are representative figures from the working population of Paris.

373
00:23:52,140 --> 00:23:53,120
Yeah, I think so.

374
00:23:53,280 --> 00:23:56,000
I mean, and we'll talk about in the second half about what the population of Paris think

375
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,480
of it, but I think you're absolutely right that basically they are pretty representative

376
00:23:59,080 --> 00:24:01,960
of the city and of the streets, I guess.

377
00:24:02,060 --> 00:24:03,320
It is the vengeance of the streets.

378
00:24:03,460 --> 00:24:06,880
That's what historians who are more sympathetic to the September Massacre say.

379
00:24:07,300 --> 00:24:11,700
On the bigwigs, the bigwigs know this is happening, but they don't do anything about it.

380
00:24:12,100 --> 00:24:18,180
Danton says to Brissot, Durandat leader, the deaths are an indispensable sacrifice to appease

381
00:24:18,180 --> 00:24:19,040
the people of Paris.

382
00:24:19,340 --> 00:24:19,380
Yeah.

383
00:24:20,040 --> 00:24:20,440
Yeah.

384
00:24:22,140 --> 00:24:26,400
I mean, the husband of Madame Roulant, the great sort of linchpin of the Girondin sort

385
00:24:26,400 --> 00:24:32,000
of social circle, he says the people terrible in its vengeance is exercising a kind of justice.

386
00:24:32,260 --> 00:24:35,600
You know, they're making excuses for it and kind of, I mean, they do nothing about it.

387
00:24:35,720 --> 00:24:40,040
That association of terror with a kind of justice, I think is exactly what's happening.

388
00:24:40,240 --> 00:24:42,860
Yeah, I think that was very French Revolution, isn't it?

389
00:24:43,180 --> 00:24:47,200
So that's the first day, but of course it's just the first several days.

390
00:24:47,400 --> 00:24:49,360
The next day you get to Monday the 3rd.

391
00:24:49,460 --> 00:24:51,540
The men at the Abbey prison.

392
00:24:51,540 --> 00:24:53,480
The first prison to be targeted.

393
00:24:53,740 --> 00:24:57,000
They're there for about 24 hours working away.

394
00:24:57,180 --> 00:24:59,940
And meanwhile, other men have moved on to other prisons.

395
00:25:00,200 --> 00:25:03,200
There's a seminary called Saint-Femin, which holds priests.

396
00:25:03,700 --> 00:25:05,620
There's a convent called Saint-Bernard.

397
00:25:05,820 --> 00:25:10,160
There's an asylum at Bicetre, which holds petty criminals.

398
00:25:10,480 --> 00:25:16,000
Perhaps most shockingly, the Saupetrière women's hospice, which holds prostitutes.

399
00:25:16,060 --> 00:25:18,180
And people have been joking, haven't they?

400
00:25:18,240 --> 00:25:21,060
We should send Marie Antoinette to the Saupetrière.

401
00:25:21,060 --> 00:25:28,840
So there, we're not talking about priests or courtiers or royalist journalists.

402
00:25:29,260 --> 00:25:34,380
These people are actually poor, petty criminals, prostitutes and so on.

403
00:25:34,500 --> 00:25:36,520
And this is, I think, where it gets particularly shocking.

404
00:25:36,640 --> 00:25:40,000
So Bicetre, they killed probably 150, 160 people.

405
00:25:40,280 --> 00:25:41,940
A lot of them are very young.

406
00:25:42,040 --> 00:25:43,940
About 40 of them are probably under 18.

407
00:25:44,320 --> 00:25:48,720
One of them is 12, two of them are 13, three of them are 14 and so on.

408
00:25:48,940 --> 00:25:49,800
Saint-Bernard.

409
00:25:49,800 --> 00:25:53,520
The people who were killed there, perhaps 70 of them, they're forgers.

410
00:25:54,220 --> 00:25:57,360
And the Sainte-Colotte hate forgers because of the paper money.

411
00:25:57,600 --> 00:25:59,680
They think the paper money is all a plot.

412
00:26:00,220 --> 00:26:04,740
They think that the forgers have been working with counter-revolutionaries to undermine

413
00:26:04,740 --> 00:26:06,880
it and to drive up grain prices.

414
00:26:07,300 --> 00:26:09,160
So if you're a forger, you've got to go.

415
00:26:09,360 --> 00:26:13,720
So David Andress, who's written a wonderful book on the terror, he comments

416
00:26:13,720 --> 00:26:14,500
on this laconically.

417
00:26:14,600 --> 00:26:18,420
September 1792 was not a good time to be a captured forger.

418
00:26:18,860 --> 00:26:19,780
And that is put in the book.

419
00:26:19,780 --> 00:26:20,400
I'm putting it mildly.

420
00:26:20,460 --> 00:26:22,360
And not a good time to be a prostitute.

421
00:26:22,500 --> 00:26:24,780
So Saul-Petriere, probably 40 prostitutes.

422
00:26:24,920 --> 00:26:26,640
Or indeed a criminal generally, right?

423
00:26:26,700 --> 00:26:31,220
Because you were talking about how throughout the revolutionary period, people have been

424
00:26:31,220 --> 00:26:37,160
assuming that criminals are in association with counter-revolutionary forces.

425
00:26:37,680 --> 00:26:43,540
And this basically seems to be why people are targeting people who are in for criminality

426
00:26:43,540 --> 00:26:45,020
rather than for political crimes.

427
00:26:45,280 --> 00:26:45,540
Yeah.

428
00:26:45,560 --> 00:26:49,760
I think actually, Tom, since 1789, maybe I don't know enough about pre-revolutionary.

429
00:26:49,760 --> 00:26:55,140
But I think certainly since 1789, you know, we talked about the grande peur, the great

430
00:26:55,140 --> 00:26:56,100
fear in the countryside.

431
00:26:56,360 --> 00:27:00,020
And that's a huge fear of brigands, isn't it, who are going to ride over the horizon

432
00:27:00,020 --> 00:27:03,620
and trample your crops and burn because they're working with the local aristocrats.

433
00:27:03,860 --> 00:27:08,540
Well, I think in Paris, there has been the same thing with criminals, you know, an anxiety

434
00:27:08,540 --> 00:27:13,620
about street crime and a belief that crime in some obscure way is connected with the

435
00:27:13,620 --> 00:27:13,920
court.

436
00:27:14,100 --> 00:27:19,280
